From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 07:19:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: derive the encoder rate control and structure from the
 device

The encoder interface hard-coded a 50 Mbps ceiling, two bitrate modes, the
AVC levels up to 5.1 and no parameter for the quality, the quantizer, the
B-frames or the layers. Read them from the encoder device's controls when
the component store builds the interface, and keep a parameter out of the
interface when the device has no control for it.

The bitrate range, the bitrate modes (constant quality is the mode that
ignores the bitrate, with an algo.quality parameter), the AVC levels up to
5.2, the quantizer range, the largest run of B-frames and the temporal
layers come from the device. The encoder passes the quantizer bounds, the
B-frames and the layers to the device, clamps the peak bitrate to the
device's range, and sets the quality of the constant quality mode, also
while the stream runs. Temporal layers are layers of P frames, so they turn
the B-frames off and constant bitrate into variable bitrate, and the base
layer's share of the bitrate sets the bitrate of each of the two layers.

The profiles the device lists reach the encoder as the V4L2 values they came
from, so a listed profile can be started, Constrained High included.

With B-frames the stream leaves the encoder in decode order, so the
component reports a finished work item without waiting for the ones before
it, except for the one that ends the stream. The input delay follows the
run of B-frames, and the latency a client reads follows the run plus the
frames the host holds back.

The device chooses the level of the stream under the ceiling the client
asks for, so the component reads the level from the first coded headers (the
AVC SPS or the HEVC general_level_idc) and reports it as the stream's profile
and level when it differs from the requested one.
